# C++ Conventions

- **Standard:** C++20. Build with `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic`.
- **Naming:** `PascalCase` types, `snake_case` functions/variables/files, `namespace nn::...`.
- **const-correctness** everywhere; `[[nodiscard]]` on results that must be used.
- Prefer `std::span` for non-owning array views (the math layer takes spans).

## Special member functions (the big 6/7)

- **Prefer Rule of Zero:** for types whose members are self-managing (`std::vector`, etc.),
  declare none of the six special members. This is established modern-C++ guidance, not a new
  C++20 rule; `Tensor` follows it because `std::vector` owns its storage.
- **Rule of Five:** only for types that manually own a resource (e.g. a future GPU buffer) —
  then define dtor + both copy + both move ops. Mark moves `noexcept`.
- Use `= default` / `= delete` to state intent. Never leave copy/move implicit on a
  resource-owning type.

## Polymorphism

- Abstract base = pure virtual methods + `virtual ~Base() = default;`.
- Concrete overrides use `override`; mark `final` when not further derived.
- Own polymorphic objects via `std::unique_ptr<Base>`; pass by reference/pointer (never by
  value — avoids slicing).
- Keep hierarchies shallow (one abstract base, then concretes); prefer composition otherwise.

## Layer paradigm

- `nn::math`: free pure functions only — no classes/state.
- `nn::core`/`nn::api`: OO. Objects call into `nn::math` for reusable numerical primitives;
  small stateful operations tied to an object may stay with that object.

Full detail + examples: `docs/CPP_CONVENTIONS.md`.
